{"cuisine/thai-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/thai-chinese-cuisine","n":"Thai-Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Thailand","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Thai-Chinese cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"thai-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thai-Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao","n":"Mindanao cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Philippines","intro":[["Filipino cuisine includes the food traditions of more than a hundred ethnolinguistic groups across the Philippine archipelago. Most widely known Filipino dishes come from the culinary traditions of groups such as the Ilocano, Pangasinan, Kapampangan, Tagalog, Bicolano, Visayan, Chavacano, and Maranao communities. The dishes associated with these groups evolved over the centuries from a largely indigenous (largely Austronesian) base shared with maritime Southeast Asia with varied influences from Chinese, Spanish, and American cuisines, in line with the major waves of influence that had enriched the cultures of the archipelago, and adapted using indigenous ingredients to meet local preferences."],["Dishes range from a simple meal of fried salted fish and rice to curries, paellas, and cozidos of Iberian origin made for fiestas. Popular dishes include lechón (whole roasted pig), longganisa (Philippine sausage), tapa (cured beef), torta (omelette), adobo (vinegar and soy sauce-based stew), kaldereta (meat stewed in tomato sauce and liver paste), mechado (larded beef in soy and tomato sauce), pochero (beef and bananas in tomato sauce), afritada (chicken or beef and vegetables simmered in tomato sauce), kare-kare (oxtail and vegetables cooked in peanut sauce), pinakbet (kabocha squash, eggplant, beans, okra, bitter melon, and tomato stew flavored with shrimp paste), sinigang (meat or seafood with vegetables in sour broth), pancit (noodles), and lumpia (fresh or fried spring rolls)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"filipino-cuisine-mindanao"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_cuisine#Mindanao"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":19,"links":[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"],["Aparon","dish/aparon"],["Banana ketchup","dish/banana-ketchup"],["Champorado","dish/champorado"],["Classic Savory","dish/classic-savory"],["Filipino barbecue","dish/filipino-barbecue"],["Food for the gods","dish/food-for-the-gods"],["Isaw","dish/isaw"],["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"],["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"],["Mango float","dish/mango-float"],["Palapa (condiment)","dish/palapa-condiment"],["Pastil","dish/pastil"],["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Pilipit","dish/pilipit"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"],["Sushi bake","dish/sushi-bake"],["Tortang talong","dish/tortang-talong"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Garcinia binucao","ingredient/garcinia-binucao"],["Pigeon pea","ingredient/pigeon-pea"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tabliya","ingredient/tabliya"],["Jackfruit","ingredient/jackfruit"],["Banana pith","ingredient/banana-pith"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Duck egg","ingredient/duck-egg"],["Duck eggs","ingredient/duck-eggs"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Carabao mango","ingredient/carabao-mango"],["Condensed milk","ingredient/condensed-milk"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/88/Philippine_Food.jpg/500px-Philippine_Food.jpg","w":500,"h":225,"by":"Philippine Department of Tourism","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippine_Food.jpg"}},"ingredient/mentha-pulegium":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mentha-pulegium","n":"Mentha pulegium","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Mentha pulegium, commonly (European) pennyroyal, or pennyrile, also called mosquito plant and pudding grass, is a species of flowering plant in the mint family, Lamiaceae, native to Europe, North Africa, and the Middle East. Crushed pennyroyal leaves emit a very strong fragrance similar to spearmint. Pennyroyal is a traditional folk remedy, emmenagogue, abortifacient, and culinary herb, but is toxic to the liver and has caused some deaths. European pennyroyal is related to an American species, Hedeoma pulegioides. Though they differ in genera, they share similar chemical properties."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mentha-pulegium"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mentha_pulegium"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Babbouche","dish/babbouche"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Elettaria cardamomum","ingredient/elettaria-cardamomum"],["Liquorice","ingredient/liquorice"],["Ras el hanout","ingredient/ras-el-hanout"],["Snail","ingredient/snail"],["Thymus","ingredient/thymus"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/7e/Gardenology.org-IMG_2751_rbgs11jan.jpg/500px-Gardenology.org-IMG_2751_rbgs11jan.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Raffi Kojian","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gardenology.org-IMG_2751_rbgs11jan.jpg"}},"ingredient/common-fig":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/common-fig","n":"Common fig","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The fig is the edible fruit of Ficus carica (the common fig), a species of tree or shrub in the flowering plant family Moraceae, native to the Mediterranean region, and to western and southern Asia. It has been cultivated since ancient times and is now widely grown throughout the world. Ficus carica is the type species of the genus Ficus, which comprises over 800 tropical and subtropical plant species."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-common-fig"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_fig"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Lady Baltimore cake","dish/lady-baltimore-cake"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Birnbrot","dish/birnbrot"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Candied fruit","ingredient/candied-fruit"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Pear","ingredient/pear"],["Cherry","ingredient/cherry"],["Meringue","ingredient/meringue"],["Pecan","ingredient/pecan"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2e/Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg/500px-Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg","w":500,"h":767,"by":"Trew, C.J","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg"}},"ingredient/dark-soy-sauce":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/dark-soy-sauce","n":"Dark soy sauce","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["In Chinese cuisine, dark soy sauce is a dark-coloured soy sauce used mainly for adding colour and flavour to dishes. It is richer, slightly thicker, and less salty than other types of soy sauce. As the Chinese name lǎo chōu (lit. 'old extract') suggests, it is also aged longer. It is often sweetened by adding molasses or other sweetening agents. Dark soy sauce is often used in stews, stir-fries, and sauces. It is used in dishes requiring colours, such as red-cooked dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-dark-soy-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_soy_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Cambodian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/cambodian-chinese-cuisine",[["Lort cha","dish/lort-cha"]]],["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Char siu","dish/char-siu"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean sprouts","ingredient/bean-sprouts"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Morning glory","ingredient/morning-glory"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pickled bean curd","ingredient/pickled-bean-curd"],["Pig blood","ingredient/pig-blood"],["Radish","ingredient/radish"],["Rice noodles","ingredient/rice-noodles"],["Five-spice powder","ingredient/five-spice-powder"],["Fortified wine","ingredient/fortified-wine"],["Hoisin sauce","ingredient/hoisin-sauce"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Maltose","ingredient/maltose"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1f/Dark_soy_sauce.png/500px-Dark_soy_sauce.png","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Maumivi","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dark_soy_sauce.png"}},"ingredient/tarragon":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tarragon","n":"Tarragon","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Tarragon (Artemisia dracunculus), also known as estragon, is a species of perennial herb in the family Asteraceae. It is widespread in the wild across much of Eurasia and North America and is cultivated for culinary and medicinal purposes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tarragon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarragon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence",[["Green goddess dressing","dish/green-goddess-dressing"]]],["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california",[["Green goddess dressing","dish/green-goddess-dressing"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anethum","ingredient/anethum"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Petroselinum","ingredient/petroselinum"],["Plum","ingredient/plum"],["Prunus cerasifera","ingredient/prunus-cerasifera"],["Prunus vachuschtii","ingredient/prunus-vachuschtii"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Tkemali","ingredient/tkemali"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/6/6c/Estragon_1511.jpg","w":500,"h":396,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Ies assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estragon_1511.jpg"}},"ingredient/shellfish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/shellfish","n":"Shellfish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Shellfish, in colloquial and fisheries usage, are exoskeleton-bearing aquatic invertebrates used as food, including various species of molluscs, crustaceans, and echinoderms. Although most kinds of shellfish are harvested from saltwater environments, some are found in freshwater. In addition, a few species of land crabs are eaten, for example Cardisoma guanhumi in the Caribbean. Shellfish are among the most common food allergens."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-shellfish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shellfish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Chilean cuisine","cuisine/chilean-cuisine",[["Curanto","dish/curanto"]]],["Neapolitan cuisine","cuisine/neapolitan-cuisine",[["Crocchè","dish/crocche"]]],["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine",[["Crocchè","dish/crocche"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brown sauce","ingredient/brown-sauce"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/37/Oysters_p1040741.jpg/500px-Oysters_p1040741.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"David Monniaux","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oysters_p1040741.jpg"}},"ingredient/qatiq":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/qatiq","n":"Qatiq","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Qatiq is a fermented milk product from the Turkic countries and Bulgaria. It is a more solid form of yogurt than ayran."]],"s