-cracker":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/graham-cracker","n":"Graham cracker","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A graham cracker is a sweet cracker made with graham flour that originated in the United States in the mid-19th century, with commercial development from about 1880, and mass production beginning in 1898 by The National Biscuit Company. It is eaten as a snack food, usually honey- or cinnamon-flavored, and is used as an ingredient in some foods, e.g., in the graham cracker crust for cheesecakes and pies."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-graham-cracker"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_cracker"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Strawberry delight","dish/strawberry-delight"]]],["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states",[["Moon Pie","dish/moon-pie"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Marshmallow","ingredient/marshmallow"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"],["Gelatin","ingredient/gelatin"],["Gelatine dessert","ingredient/gelatine-dessert"],["Strawberry","ingredient/strawberry"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"],["Whipped topping","ingredient/whipped-topping"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/87/Graham-Cracker-Stack.jpg/500px-Graham-Cracker-Stack.jpg","w":500,"h":359,"by":"Evan-Amos","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graham-Cracker-Stack.jpg"}},"ingredient/animal-fat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/animal-fat","n":"Animal fat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Animal fats are lipids derived from animals which are used by the animal for a multitude of functions, or can be used by humans for dietary, sanitary, and cosmetic purposes. Depending on the temperature of the fat, it can change between a solid state and a liquid (oil) state. Chemically, both fats and oils are composed of triglycerides. Although many animal parts and secretions may yield oil, in commercial practice, oil is extracted primarily from rendered tissue fats from livestock animals like pigs, chickens and cows. Dairy products yield animal fat and oil products such as butter."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-animal-fat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_fat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"]]],["Kyrgyz cuisine","cuisine/kyrgyz-cuisine",[["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"]]],["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",[["Orama (dish)","dish/orama-dish"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brassica","ingredient/brassica"],["Cucurbita","ingredient/cucurbita"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/79/Reuzel.jpg/500px-Reuzel.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Rasbak","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reuzel.jpg"}},"ingredient/freshwater-fish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/freshwater-fish","n":"Freshwater fish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Freshwater fish are fish species that spend some or all of their lives in bodies of fresh water such as rivers, lakes, ponds and inland wetlands, where the salinity is less than 1.05%. These environments differ from marine habitats in many ways, especially the difference in levels of osmolarity. To survive in fresh water, fish need a range of physiological adaptations."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-freshwater-fish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freshwater_fish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Pekasam","dish/pekasam"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a6/TincaTincaWeerribben.JPG/500px-TincaTincaWeerribben.JPG","w":500,"h":254,"by":"Piet Spaans","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TincaTincaWeerribben.JPG"}},"dish/pierre-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pierre-restaurant","n":"Pierre (restaurant)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Pierre is a closed French cuisine restaurant which was situated on the 25th floor of the Mandarin Oriental hotel in Hong Kong. It opened in October 2006 after a major renovation to the hotel and was Pierre Gagnaire’s pied-à-terre in Hong Kong. It replaced Vong's, which replaced Pierrot (1979–1997), a classic French restaurant. After 14 years in service, Pierre closed on 31 July 2020."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pierre-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_(restaurant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","ref":"site/pardonyourfrench-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","ref":"site/lacuisinedegeraldine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","